      "problem": "${({(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2})}^{-2}=$",
      "worked_solution": "To raise a power to another power, we simply multiply the exponents:\n\n$$\n{({(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2})}^{-2}={(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2\\cdot (-2)}={(\\frac{1}{6})}^{-4}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate ${(\\frac{1}{6})}^{-4},$ we find the reciprocal of the base $\\frac{1}{6}$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{-4}={6}^{4}\n$$",
